WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:07.820
I'm here to share a truth with you.

00:00:08.700 --> 00:00:12.380
And so I am ready to share my truth.

00:00:12.420 --> 00:00:15.100
And the truth is,

00:00:15.100 --> 00:00:18.540
I love scam artists.

00:00:19.060 --> 00:00:21.100
I mean, I don't love scam artists,

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:22.860
I love learning about them.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:27.140
I love reading books about them,
watching documentaries about them,

00:00:27.180 --> 00:00:31.140
I love learning about the teleshopping
queen Wanna Marchi in Italy.

00:00:31.180 --> 00:00:33.900
I like learning how to avoid
creeps like Simon Leviev,

00:00:33.940 --> 00:00:36.020
you know, the "Tinder Swindler."

00:00:36.060 --> 00:00:40.420
I even liked watching both Fyre Festival
documentaries the day they came out

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:42.660
and have listened
to every single radio edit

00:00:42.700 --> 00:00:44.740
of every Billy McFarland interview

00:00:44.740 --> 00:00:46.460
that exists in this dimension.

00:00:46.500 --> 00:00:48.580
I'm, like, obsessed.

00:00:48.620 --> 00:00:51.580
And I think we can all learn a lot
from scam artists, you know,

00:00:51.620 --> 00:00:55.020
not like how to live
your life or something,

00:00:55.020 --> 00:00:58.180
but specifically how money
functions in our society.

00:00:58.180 --> 00:01:01.100
So if you're ready to go
on this journey with me today,

00:01:01.100 --> 00:01:04.980
I would like to teach you money lessons
from famous scam artists.

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:08.020
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:01:08.060 --> 00:01:09.300
(Applause)

00:01:09.620 --> 00:01:11.420
So how did I get here?

00:01:11.420 --> 00:01:14.380
Well, I'm an artist,
you know, minus the scam part.

00:01:14.420 --> 00:01:16.940
I'm a poet and a filmmaker specifically.

00:01:16.980 --> 00:01:20.700
And when I started my arts journey
full-time over a decade ago,

00:01:20.700 --> 00:01:23.940
I was unprepared for the amount
of grants I'd have to write.

00:01:23.940 --> 00:01:26.340
Turns out that a lot of being
a professional artist

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:28.420
is fundraising for your own salary.

00:01:29.340 --> 00:01:31.100
So I got good at grants.

00:01:31.140 --> 00:01:32.580
I got so good at grants,

00:01:32.580 --> 00:01:34.900
I started writing grants for other people.

00:01:34.940 --> 00:01:36.060
In one year alone,

00:01:36.100 --> 00:01:37.940
through writing grants for folks,

00:01:37.940 --> 00:01:41.900
running people's fundraising campaigns,
supporting mutual aid efforts,

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:44.860
I was able to help move
1.3 million dollars

00:01:44.860 --> 00:01:48.660
directly into the hands of Black
trans individuals and organizations.

00:01:48.660 --> 00:01:53.220
(Applause and cheers)

00:01:53.940 --> 00:01:56.420
I started consulting
with philanthropic organizations

00:01:56.460 --> 00:01:58.700
on the best way to support
targeted communities,

00:01:58.740 --> 00:02:01.500
and I teach reparations frameworks
all across the country.

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:03.940
So I know why people give money.

00:02:03.980 --> 00:02:06.620
And honestly, a lot of it
is great storytelling,

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:09.580
and scam artists are really good
at spinning a narrative.

00:02:09.900 --> 00:02:12.940
Now, the first famous scam artist
I'd like to tell you all about

00:02:12.940 --> 00:02:15.020
is the infamous Elizabeth Holmes.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.740
So Elizabeth Holmes, who founded Theranos,

00:02:17.780 --> 00:02:21.340
was able to rake in
about a nine-billion-dollar valuation

00:02:21.340 --> 00:02:23.420
for a product that didn't even work.

00:02:24.020 --> 00:02:27.540
For those of you that don't remember
Elizabeth Holmes, because you somehow,

00:02:27.580 --> 00:02:30.900
I don't know, like, missed
the competing podcast, the book,

00:02:30.900 --> 00:02:33.580
the documentary and the very public trial,

00:02:33.620 --> 00:02:35.820
let me remind you who she is.

00:02:35.860 --> 00:02:40.260
So she started Theranos under the premise
that they would be creating a device

00:02:40.300 --> 00:02:43.620
that could read hundreds of medical tests

00:02:43.620 --> 00:02:45.700
through a single drop of blood.

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:49.780
Now she surrounded herself
with government officials,

00:02:49.780 --> 00:02:54.020
venture capitalists and a bevy
of high-gloss magazine covers.

00:02:54.020 --> 00:02:59.300
What she did not surround herself with,
though, were medical experts as peers,

00:02:59.340 --> 00:03:00.500
right?

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.060
And so for many people,

00:03:02.100 --> 00:03:04.500
and for many of them
that came close to her,

00:03:04.500 --> 00:03:06.100
they could realize pretty quickly

00:03:06.100 --> 00:03:08.500
that what she was selling
just wasn't real.

00:03:08.500 --> 00:03:13.260
And so for most folks, it's very clear
that Elizabeth Holmes is a scammer.

00:03:13.300 --> 00:03:16.860
But have you ever listened
to the rationalizations of her funders?

00:03:16.900 --> 00:03:18.380
That stuff is gold.

00:03:18.820 --> 00:03:21.100
So out of all of her investors,

00:03:21.140 --> 00:03:24.060
my favorite interview
is one with Don Lucas Sr.

00:03:24.100 --> 00:03:26.780
So Don Lucas Sr.,
who gave her millions of dollars,

00:03:26.820 --> 00:03:30.540
was asked to explain,
"Why did you give her so much money?"

00:03:30.580 --> 00:03:34.260
And so he said, "Well, her great-
grandfather was an entrepreneur."

00:03:34.260 --> 00:03:37.340
And it turns out the hospital
near where her family lives

00:03:37.380 --> 00:03:39.260
is named for her great-uncle.

00:03:39.260 --> 00:03:43.140
Yeah, so she was supposedly
the best of both worlds

00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:48.580
and had supposedly learned both medicine
and entrepreneurship through osmosis.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:50.500
(Laughter)

00:03:50.540 --> 00:03:53.500
Another gem of an interview
was with Tim Draper.

00:03:53.500 --> 00:03:57.780
So Tim Draper pushed back against
an interviewer who asked,

00:03:57.820 --> 00:04:01.620
"Now, do you think that Elizabeth Holmes,
being a family friend of yours,

00:04:01.660 --> 00:04:04.540
had anything to do with you
giving her so much money?"

00:04:04.540 --> 00:04:05.740
He scoffed.

00:04:05.780 --> 00:04:07.500
I mean, he's a professional.

00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:09.380
He doesn't do trivial things like that.

00:04:09.420 --> 00:04:11.660
He was a first investor
in Tesla and Skype.

00:04:11.700 --> 00:04:14.780
He just knows who's going
to go the distance.

00:04:14.780 --> 00:04:19.260
And as we know, Theranos tanked,
horribly and publicly,

00:04:19.300 --> 00:04:23.540
after opening 40 centers
at Arizona-based Walgreens locations

00:04:23.580 --> 00:04:26.780
and endangering thousands of people
who got blood tests

00:04:26.780 --> 00:04:29.060
that the company knew
were going to be faulty.

00:04:29.780 --> 00:04:32.140
A lie we get told again and again,

00:04:32.180 --> 00:04:34.820
is that broke folks are bad at money.

00:04:35.220 --> 00:04:38.500
It's working-class folks who make
bad decisions about money.

00:04:38.500 --> 00:04:40.700
This brings us to our first lesson.

00:04:40.740 --> 00:04:44.180
People with wealth aren't better
at making decisions.

00:04:44.180 --> 00:04:47.740
They get to make more bad decisions
without it hurting them.

00:04:48.980 --> 00:04:53.500
(Applause)

00:04:53.540 --> 00:04:57.140
With the added bonus of being able
to cause extensive harm

00:04:57.140 --> 00:04:59.300
to the people over whom they wield power.

00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:02.860
When broke folks make
bad decisions about money,

00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:05.100
it only impacts them,

00:05:05.140 --> 00:05:06.860
not whole ecosystems.

00:05:08.340 --> 00:05:11.220
The next famous scam artist
I'd like to talk to you all about

00:05:11.260 --> 00:05:13.260
is the story of Margaret and Walter Keane.

00:05:13.260 --> 00:05:14.700
Now in the 1960s,

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.820
Walter got famous for painting.

00:05:16.860 --> 00:05:18.500
But guess what Walter doesn't do?

00:05:18.540 --> 00:05:20.300
Walter doesn’t paint, OK?

00:05:20.340 --> 00:05:24.140
He steals all of the work
from Margaret, right?

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.620
And these beautiful pieces,

00:05:26.660 --> 00:05:29.540
known for their resonant features
of children with big eyes,

00:05:29.580 --> 00:05:31.540
were shown off at clubs,

00:05:31.540 --> 00:05:33.540
at festivals, even at the UN.

00:05:33.580 --> 00:05:35.620
They made him millions of dollars.

00:05:35.620 --> 00:05:39.780
He even had celebrity clientele
such as the Kennedy family.

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:43.540
Because of the difficulty
of being taken seriously

00:05:43.580 --> 00:05:45.300
as an artist who was a woman,

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:48.420
it was easy to erase Margaret
from her work.

00:05:48.740 --> 00:05:50.700
Walter even was able to convince her

00:05:50.700 --> 00:05:53.580
that this was the best way
to get her work seen at all.

00:05:53.620 --> 00:05:56.500
Which brings us to our next point
and lesson for the day.

00:05:57.180 --> 00:05:58.700
In an unjust society,

00:05:58.740 --> 00:06:01.500
our social identities
are often used as markers

00:06:01.540 --> 00:06:05.020
to decide whether we are worthy
of being taken seriously

00:06:05.060 --> 00:06:08.460
or deserving of financial
and other resources, OK?

00:06:08.500 --> 00:06:11.780
And when I'm saying other resources,
I'm saying sometimes it's money,

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.180
sometimes it's food,

00:06:13.180 --> 00:06:16.100
sometimes it's housing,
sometimes it's water.

00:06:17.780 --> 00:06:19.540
The next story that's coming up to me

00:06:19.580 --> 00:06:22.300
actually is not a scam
artist story per se,

00:06:22.300 --> 00:06:24.180
but a triumph over scam artist story.

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:26.140
If I can diverge just for a little bit,

00:06:26.140 --> 00:06:27.500
is that OK, yeah?

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:30.620
And so I'm thinking so much
about the story of Carlette Duffy.

00:06:30.620 --> 00:06:34.020
So Carlette Duffy is a Black woman
based in Indianapolis.

00:06:34.020 --> 00:06:37.940
And in 2020, she decided
that she needed to refinance her home.

00:06:37.980 --> 00:06:40.980
And so when she did that,
she had two appraisal teams come down,

00:06:40.980 --> 00:06:43.780
and they both undervalued
her home extensively.

00:06:43.780 --> 00:06:46.980
The lowest appraisal
being at 110,000 dollars.

00:06:46.980 --> 00:06:48.580
So she had an idea.

00:06:48.580 --> 00:06:52.300
She took down every picture
of her family from her home.

00:06:52.300 --> 00:06:56.740
She removed every single piece of artwork
that depicted Black people.

00:06:56.740 --> 00:07:00.820
She removed all of her hair products
and she called a friend,

00:07:00.860 --> 00:07:04.620
a white man that news outlets
simply refer to as "Hank."

00:07:05.020 --> 00:07:08.540
Well, don't you know that when Hank
stood in for her at the next appraisal,

00:07:08.580 --> 00:07:13.700
her home went from being worth
110,000 dollars to 259,000 dollars.

00:07:14.260 --> 00:07:16.220
Does that sound like a scam to you?

00:07:16.260 --> 00:07:18.660
Because it sounds like a scam to me.

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:21.780
And in that story,
what I think also comes up,

00:07:21.780 --> 00:07:23.500
is how common that scam is.

00:07:23.540 --> 00:07:25.860
And it is one that's based
in redlining practices

00:07:25.860 --> 00:07:27.300
and white supremacy culture.

00:07:28.180 --> 00:07:31.580
Now the next and last scam artist
that I would like to share with y'all

00:07:31.620 --> 00:07:35.220
is the amazing Anna Sorokin,
aka Anna Delvey, OK?

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:37.660
Yeah, so some of y'all are fans,
I could tell, OK.

00:07:37.700 --> 00:07:39.060
Hide your wallets, OK?

00:07:39.100 --> 00:07:40.220
And so ...

00:07:40.980 --> 00:07:43.420
So Anna Delvey pretended
to be a German heiress,

00:07:43.460 --> 00:07:45.900
and she almost, every space she went into,

00:07:45.940 --> 00:07:51.100
she took the New York scene of financial
institutions and socialites for a ride

00:07:51.140 --> 00:07:55.140
that would cost them 275,000 dollars.

00:07:55.140 --> 00:07:59.380
She lived in sprawling apartments,
she flew on private jets,

00:07:59.420 --> 00:08:01.500
she vacationed on yachts,

00:08:01.540 --> 00:08:04.700
she ate meals that cost
as much as my rent.

00:08:04.700 --> 00:08:06.660
She said she was rich.

00:08:06.700 --> 00:08:08.020
She looked the part.

00:08:08.020 --> 00:08:11.780
And so as a result, people felt compelled
to give her more money.

00:08:12.260 --> 00:08:13.980
Think of how ridiculous that sounds.

00:08:13.980 --> 00:08:16.300
Because she said she was rich,

00:08:16.340 --> 00:08:17.860
she looked the part,

00:08:17.860 --> 00:08:21.380
people thought she also
needed their money.

00:08:21.420 --> 00:08:22.780
Whether a gift or a loan,

00:08:22.820 --> 00:08:25.260
Anna would always promise
to pay people back

00:08:25.260 --> 00:08:29.140
that decided they wanted to help her
in supposedly urgent situations

00:08:29.140 --> 00:08:32.020
when she wasn't able to access
her totally fictional

00:08:32.060 --> 00:08:34.380
but supposedly sprawling fortune.

00:08:35.380 --> 00:08:38.300
What Anna's story reminds me
is this next point.

00:08:38.820 --> 00:08:41.980
Again, in an unjust system,
in an unjust society,

00:08:42.020 --> 00:08:44.620
those who are seen as being able
to perform wealth

00:08:44.660 --> 00:08:48.700
are more likely to get funded
than those in actual financial need.

00:08:48.740 --> 00:08:52.100
Not only is this a gross practice,
it's an unsustainable one.

00:08:52.100 --> 00:08:55.500
In 2014, a study found
that overconsumption

00:08:55.540 --> 00:08:59.420
and economic inequality leads
to the collapse of civilizations.

00:08:59.900 --> 00:09:03.140
While having a lot of wealth
may be able to buy you a PR team

00:09:03.140 --> 00:09:06.380
to make you more likable
to the general population,

00:09:06.380 --> 00:09:08.460
it doesn't make you a more skilled person.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:10.100
It doesn't make you more capable,

00:09:10.100 --> 00:09:12.420
and it certainly doesn't
make you more perceptive

00:09:12.420 --> 00:09:14.700
about the sustainability of resources.

00:09:14.700 --> 00:09:17.820
This idea that wealthy people
are somehow more deserving

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.300
is one that is very pervasive

00:09:20.340 --> 00:09:22.940
and one that many different
types of people believe.

00:09:22.940 --> 00:09:25.740
In this society, because of all
the systems we live under,

00:09:25.780 --> 00:09:30.420
lots of broke folks also believe the idea
that they are somehow less worthy

00:09:30.460 --> 00:09:32.100
than their wealthy counterparts.

00:09:32.620 --> 00:09:35.420
Scam artists actually know something

00:09:35.460 --> 00:09:39.140
that most of us in this room
would deny or refuse to accept.

00:09:39.140 --> 00:09:40.980
And that is,

00:09:41.020 --> 00:09:43.620
money doesn't typically come
from hard work.

00:09:44.060 --> 00:09:46.140
Money comes to those who hoard it.

00:09:46.140 --> 00:09:48.420
Money comes to those
who are willing to exploit.

00:09:48.460 --> 00:09:50.140
Money comes to those who work hard

00:09:50.140 --> 00:09:52.300
to bury the history
of where money comes from.

00:09:52.340 --> 00:09:56.380
Billions of people live on this planet
that work hard every single day

00:09:56.420 --> 00:09:58.660
that will live and die broke.

00:09:59.380 --> 00:10:02.620
Money comes to financial institutions
like Bank of America

00:10:02.620 --> 00:10:03.940
and JPMorgan Chase,

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.900
who literally got their start
in the transatlantic slave trade

00:10:06.940 --> 00:10:09.460
and will find themselves
in the news every few years

00:10:09.500 --> 00:10:13.700
for denying loans to the very descendants
of the same people they once owned.

00:10:14.780 --> 00:10:18.140
To big tech giants like Apple and Amazon,

00:10:18.140 --> 00:10:22.020
who will pay top dollar
for a coder behind a desk in Seattle

00:10:22.060 --> 00:10:23.780
but will pay darn near nothing

00:10:23.820 --> 00:10:26.020
for the hands of those
that work tirelessly

00:10:26.060 --> 00:10:29.860
to mine the materials
that make our devices function

00:10:29.860 --> 00:10:32.380
and to deliver our packages cheaply.

00:10:32.700 --> 00:10:36.740
To living in a society where it is legal
to pay both disabled workers

00:10:36.780 --> 00:10:39.780
and essential workers
less than minimum wage.

00:10:39.820 --> 00:10:43.900
The way we deal with money
in this society is the real scam.

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:46.780
And while the scam artists
that I'm talking about

00:10:46.820 --> 00:10:51.100
have had their tricks and tools exposed
for the whole world to see,

00:10:51.140 --> 00:10:54.180
there are scams in our faces
every single day.

00:10:55.860 --> 00:10:57.860
So what do I want you to get out of today?

00:10:58.500 --> 00:11:00.140
I want you to consider

00:11:00.180 --> 00:11:04.500
that there are scams that we are asked
to agree not to see all the time.

00:11:05.180 --> 00:11:07.740
And I want you to think
about in your personal life,

00:11:07.740 --> 00:11:10.900
in your interpersonal life
and in your greater community life,

00:11:10.940 --> 00:11:15.300
what are some scams
that we are asked to pretend are OK

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:17.780
and understanding that when we do that,

00:11:17.820 --> 00:11:20.220
we set up a lot
of our communities to fail.

00:11:20.980 --> 00:11:23.940
As an artist, I have
the distinct pleasure and joy

00:11:23.980 --> 00:11:26.460
of helping people to dream possibilities

00:11:26.460 --> 00:11:28.940
that don't already exist on this planet.

00:11:28.980 --> 00:11:31.140
So today I'm asking you to dream with me.

00:11:31.460 --> 00:11:33.940
I want you to dream a possible world

00:11:33.980 --> 00:11:37.380
in which we delegate resources like money

00:11:37.420 --> 00:11:39.380
from a place of community care.

00:11:39.420 --> 00:11:43.020
What do actual ethical practices
around money look like?

00:11:43.540 --> 00:11:48.220
Money in this society
is often used to prove morality,

00:11:48.220 --> 00:11:50.820
competency, wisdom,

00:11:50.820 --> 00:11:53.420
when really it's just a tool we made up.

00:11:53.940 --> 00:11:55.820
And we get to decide how we use it,

00:11:55.860 --> 00:11:59.300
how we move forward with it
or without it for the collective good.

00:11:59.620 --> 00:12:00.780
Thank you.

00:12:00.820 --> 00:12:02.940
(Applause and cheers)

